WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.560
Last time, water crossed between two rooms until every litre held the same number of

2
00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:09.940
stuck guests. Now a blood test reads low plasma sodium.

3
00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:12.660
Has this body lost sodium?

4
00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:20.420
Plasma sodium is a concentration: sodium per litre of water.

5
00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:25.300
So the reading has two parts, the sodium and the water it sits in.

6
00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:28.500
Change either one, and the needle moves.

7
00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:32.160
Take the litre of solute-free water again.

8
00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:34.900
No sodium left the body.

9
00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:40.720
But the litre spread through all body water, and diluted the sodium outside cells.

10
00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:49.140
In the model, plasma sodium falls from 140 to about 136.7 mmol per L.

11
00:00:50.283 --> 00:00:52.603
Now a stranger case, as bookkeeping.

12
00:00:53.983 --> 00:00:58.763
Remove 600 mOsm of potassium salts from the cells, and from the body.

13
00:01:00.063 --> 00:01:01.643
Not one sodium ion leaves.

14
00:01:02.983 --> 00:01:05.943
Does plasma sodium rise, fall, or stay the same?

15
00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:16.460
It falls. Potassium salts are the cells' stuck guests.

16
00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:20.040
With fewer of them, cells hold less water.

17
00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:28.560
In the model, cells shrink from 28 to about 27.3 L. That water moves outside.

18
00:01:29.650 --> 00:01:38.410
Fluid outside cells rises to about 14.7 L. Plasma sodium falls from 140 to about

19
00:01:38.410 --> 00:01:42.270
133. No sodium was lost.

20
00:01:43.550 --> 00:01:48.590
Real potassium depletion is messier, but the body still loses effective solute.

21
00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:51.430
So plasma sodium still falls.

22
00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:59.100
Here is the idea. Plasma sodium tracks all the exchangeable cation, sodium and

23
00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:01.400
potassium, per litre of body water.

24
00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:04.780
It is a ratio of cation to water.

25
00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:08.340
It is not a gauge of the body's sodium stock.

26
00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:15.920
In 1958, Isidore Edelman and colleagues tested this in 98 chronically ill patients.

27
00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:22.720
In most, they measured exchangeable sodium, exchangeable potassium and total body

28
00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:24.500
water by isotope dilution.

29
00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:30.480
Exchangeable means the part of each store that mixes with an injected isotope.

30
00:02:31.483 --> 00:02:35.003
Serum sodium correlated poorly with any single store.

31
00:02:36.263 --> 00:02:41.003
It followed sodium plus potassium per litre of body water, with a correlation

32
00:02:41.003 --> 00:02:43.263
coefficient of 0.83.

33
00:02:44.703 --> 00:02:49.503
Patients with edema, carrying excess body sodium, could still have low plasma

34
00:02:49.503 --> 00:02:50.503
sodium.

35
00:02:51.067 --> 00:02:56.907
Keep the limits. This is a correlation in sick patients, not an experiment.

36
00:02:58.287 --> 00:03:03.267
And exchangeable cation is not total cation, because much bone sodium does not

37
00:03:03.267 --> 00:03:04.267
exchange.

38
00:03:04.850 --> 00:03:09.090
So a low plasma sodium means too much water for the cation present.

39
00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:14.710
By itself, it does not show whether body sodium is low, normal or high.
